[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"API Testing Fundamentals using Postman (Manual validation)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) serve as the backbone of modern software architecture, allowing different systems to communicate. Manual API testing using Postman is an essential skill for QA engineers to validate backend functionality, data integrity, and response behavior without relying on a graphical user interface."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding API Basics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into Postman, you must understand the structure of an HTTP request and response. An API request consists of an endpoint (URL), an HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE), headers, and optionally a body. The response contains a status code, response headers, and a payload (usually JSON)."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Setting Up Postman"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Download and install the Postman desktop application from the official website."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a free workspace to organize your collections and environment variables."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Creating Your First GET Request"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To test retrieving data from a server, you will use a GET request. Follow these steps to perform a manual validation:"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open Postman and click on the New button (+) to open a new request tab."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select GET from the HTTP method dropdown next to the URL bar."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enter a sample public API endpoint, such as https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Click the Send button to execute the request."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Validating the Response"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the request is sent, inspect the response section at the bottom of the Postman interface. Manual validation involves checking three key elements:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"HTTP Status Code: Verify that the status is 200 OK, indicating a successful operation."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Response Time: Check how long the server took to respond (e.g., under 500ms for optimal performance)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Response Body: Verify that the returned JSON payload contains the expected keys and data types."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "Leanne Graham",
  "username": "Bret",
  "email": "Sincere@april.biz"
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Creating a POST Request with a Payload"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"To test data creation, you will use a POST request that sends a payload to the server. Configure the request by setting the method to POST, adding the endpoint, and providing the JSON body."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set the method to POST."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Navigate to the Body tab below the URL bar."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select the raw radio button and change the dropdown format from Text to JSON."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"{
  "title": "Postman Testing Guide",
  "body": "Learning manual API validation",
  "userId": 1
}"},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"6. Conclusion"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Manual API testing in Postman gives you direct control over request parameters, headers, and payloads. By consistently checking status codes, response headers, and body payloads, you can catch functional defects early in the software development lifecycle before moving on to automated testing."}]
